{
  "title": "Rate limiting for the Orders API",
  "subtitle": "feature/rate-limit → main",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "type": "intent",
      "chapter": "Why this change",
      "narration": "This merge request protects the orders endpoint from bursts of traffic, prices orders from the pricing service, and emits an event for every new order. It adds no tests, so the review rests on reading the code.",
      "summary": "Add Redis-backed rate limiting to POST /orders and publish order.created events.",
      "risk": "medium",
      "bullets": [
        "A Redis outage now fails every order request, because the limiter has no fallback",
        "publish runs after the insert, so a queue outage returns an error for an order that was saved",
        "No tests cover the limiter, the pricing lookup, or the new event"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "arch",
      "chapter": "Where it fits",
      "narration": "Requests now pass through a rate limiter backed by Redis before reaching the orders service, which writes to the database and then publishes to the event queue.",
